We investigate the bistar-shaped ground state solution for the given biharmonic Choquard type equation \begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
  \Delta^2u - \mu \nabla u = \lambda u + \frac{h^2}{|x|^2} u + \frac{F'_*(u)}{|x|^2} = 0 \quad \text{in} \ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
  \end{split}
\end{align*}under the constraints $0 < \mu < \mu_1$, $\lambda > 0$, $h > 0$, where $\mu_1$ represents the first eigenvalue of the $-\Delta$ operator on $\mathbb{R}^4$. Our analysis also takes into consideration the scenario where $f$ is a bistable cross curvature metric for which $f(u)$ is not necessarily convex in $u$. We standardize the ground state solution using a generalized symmetry property and establish its monotonicity via a generalized symmetry property and the fixed point index theory. Furthermore, we demonstrate the existence of a natural homotopy from $C^1_0(\mathbb{R}^4)$ to $C^1_0(\mathbb{B}^4)$.
